===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|harre}}, {{m|enm|herre}}, from {{m|ang|heorra||hinge; cardinal point}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*herzô||hinge}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)kerd-||to move, sway, swing, jump}}. Cognate with Scots {{m|sco|herre}}, {{m|sco|harr}}, {{m|sco|har||hinge}}, Dutch {{m|nl|harre}}, {{m|nl|her}}, {{m|nl|har||hinge}}, Icelandic {{m|is|hjarri||hinge}}, Latin {{m|la|cardō||hinge}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|harre}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dialectal}} A [[hinge]].

===Etymology 2===
Onomatopoeic.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|hardy har har}}

====Interjection====
{{en-interj}}

# A [[sound]] of [[laughter]], with a [[sarcastic]] [[connotation]].

